  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was mixed: Port Washington softened on retail and transaction count, while Hicksville surprised on the upside with strong retail performance. The pattern across both flagships masks an operational reality you need to see clearly.\n\nPort Washington revenue fell 11% day-over-day, but capacity was solid\u20145 groomers producing $591 per groomer against a 30-day baseline of $468, which is hot. The real miss is retail, down 34% with transactions off 33%, signaling a front-of-house focus gap, not a grooming problem. Add-on attach also slipped from 67% to 58%, which is a upsell discipline issue worth a quick conversation with floor leadership. Month-to-date, Port is 9 percentage points below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014that's real performance drift, not calendar. Hicksville, by contrast, is nearly on plan (\u22120.6% vs. DOW-adjusted), and yesterday's retail surge (+76%) and higher transaction count (+18%) despite fewer appointments suggests the team is converting better and working harder. Hicksville's $/groomer-day is $440 against a $376 prior-month baseline, so they're running efficient.\n\nThe concerning signal: your lapse pool across both stores totals 709 customers worth $457,582 in annual value. That's your biggest near-term revenue lever. Glen Cove has zero groomer-days scheduled for the next 7 days\u2014clarify whether that's a staffing issue, a market decision, or an oversight. Reorder three fast-movers immediately at Port Washington: Shanti's Medical Journey, Jack&Pup Braided Bully, and X-Small Treat Table (all zero inventory, 20+ monthly velocity).\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you have 11 scheduled groomer-days across Port (6) and Hicksville (5) with 15 bather hours; tomorrow drops to 8 groomer-days; next seven days 58 groomer-days total with 83 bather hours\u2014monitor Hicksville's zero bather coverage against demand."
